  ctaHeading="Replacing a Water Heater and Want the Paperwork Right?"
  ctaText="We pull the permit, do the work to code and meet the inspector. You end up with hot water and a clean record on the property."
>
  <p>
    Almost every homeowner who has replaced a water heater in California has met the same pitch:
    someone offering to do it for noticeably less, cash, this afternoon, no permit. It is tempting
    precisely because nothing visibly bad happens afterwards. The water gets hot either way. What
    you are actually buying is an undocumented gas appliance and a problem deferred to a worse
    moment.
  </p>

  <h2>Why a Permit Is Required at All</h2>
  <p>
    A water heater is not a plug-in appliance. In a typical Rocklin home it ties into the gas
    supply, vents combustion products, holds water under pressure at scalding temperature, and
    sits in a seismically active state. Get any of those wrong and the failure modes are not
    inconvenience &mdash; they are fire, carbon monoxide, flooding or a toppled tank tearing a gas
    line off its fitting in an earthquake.
  </p>
  <p>
    The permit and inspection process exists so that an independent set of eyes confirms the
    install is safe before the job is considered finished. It also creates a record attached to
    the property, which matters far more than people expect when the house changes hands or an
    insurer asks questions.
  </p>
  <p>
    Permits are administered locally, so the exact fee, the forms and the inspection process vary
    between jurisdictions. Your building department is the authority on the specifics for your
    address, and a contractor who works in your city every week will already know the local
    routine.
  </p>

  <h2>What the Inspector Actually Checks</h2>
  <p>
    Inspections are more predictable than people fear. An inspector works through a short list of
    items that cause real-world failures.
  </p>

  <h3>Seismic strapping</h3>
  <p>
    California requires storage water heaters to be braced against earthquake movement.
    Conventionally that means two straps &mdash; one in the upper portion of the tank and one in
    the lower &mdash; anchored into solid framing or masonry, not just into drywall. Inspectors
    look at strap placement, the fixings used, and whether the straps are actually tight. A single
    loose strap around the middle of the tank is one of the most common corrections written.
  </p>

  <h3>Temperature and pressure relief discharge</h3>
  <p>
    The T&amp;P valve is the safety device that prevents a water heater becoming a pressure
    vessel. Its discharge pipe has to be the correct size, run downhill to an approved termination
    where a discharge would be visible and would not scald anyone, and have no valve, no reduction
    and no threaded end that could be capped. Inspectors check the routing, the material and the
    termination point. Discharge piping that runs uphill, into a sealed container or nowhere at all
    is an automatic correction.
  </p>

  <h3>Drain pan and pan drain</h3>
  <p>
    Where a leak could damage the building &mdash; interior closets, upstairs locations, above
    finished space &mdash; the heater needs a pan beneath it, piped to a suitable drain point. A
    pan with no outlet is a bathtub waiting to overflow, so the inspector looks for where the pan
    actually drains to.
  </p>

  <h3>Thermal expansion control</h3>
  <p>
    If the water supply has a pressure regulator or a check valve, the house plumbing is a closed
    system. Heated water expands and, with nowhere to go, pressure spikes every heating cycle.
    That is what an expansion tank handles. Inspectors look for one where the system requires it,
    correctly supported and installed in the right orientation for the model.
  </p>

  <h3>Combustion air</h3>
  <p>
    An atmospheric gas water heater burns air from the room it sits in. Shut it in a sealed closet
    and it will starve, burn poorly and potentially spill combustion products into the house.
    Inspectors check that the space has adequate combustion and ventilation air, whether that is
    through the garage, through louvred openings or via ducted air. Converting a garage to a sealed
    room with the heater still in it is a classic way to fail.
  </p>

  <h3>Venting and draft</h3>
  <p>
    On a gas unit, the inspector checks vent material, diameter, slope, connector length, clearance
    to combustibles and whether the vent is correctly connected and supported. Common issues are
    crushed or disconnected flex connectors, an undersized vent after someone fitted a larger
    heater, too many elbows, and inadequate rise before the first turn. The point of all of it is
    to confirm the appliance actually draws its exhaust outside rather than into the garage.
  </p>

  <h3>Gas supply, sediment trap and shutoff</h3>
  <p>
    The gas connection needs an accessible shutoff valve, an appropriate approved connector, and a
    sediment trap (a drip leg) ahead of the appliance control to catch debris and moisture before
    it reaches the gas valve. Inspectors also look for correctly made joints and a leak test. The
    sediment trap is routinely left off by hurried installers and is one of the easiest items for
    an inspector to spot.
  </p>

  <h3>Water connections and electrical</h3>
  <p>
    Expect a look at dielectric separation where dissimilar metals meet, accessible isolation on
    the cold inlet, and &mdash; on electric and heat pump units &mdash; a proper disconnect and
    bonding.
  </p>

  <h2>What Happens If You Skip It</h2>
  <p>
    The cost of skipping a permit is almost never paid on the day. It is paid later, in one of
    these four ways.
  </p>
  <ol class="steps">
    <li><strong>Resale friction</strong> California sellers have broad disclosure obligations about the condition of a property. An unpermitted gas appliance that shows up in a buyer home inspection becomes a negotiation item at the worst possible moment &mdash; and the usual outcome is you paying to permit and correct it under time pressure, or a price reduction.</li>
    <li><strong>Insurance trouble</strong> If a water heater causes water damage, a fire or a carbon monoxide incident, the first thing an adjuster establishes is whether the installation was permitted and code compliant. Unpermitted work gives an insurer a straightforward basis to question or reduce a claim. You do not want to discover that argument exists after the damage.</li>
    <li><strong>Warranty and liability gaps</strong> Manufacturers condition warranties on correct installation. An uninspected install by someone who may not have been licensed leaves you with nobody to stand behind the work, and no documentation to prove what was done.</li>
    <li><strong>Future permits exposing it</strong> Apply to permit a remodel, a solar array or a panel upgrade and the inspector sees the whole property. Unpermitted work discovered then has to be resolved before the new project can proceed.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    And the safety point, which is the real one: strapping, relief discharge, combustion air and
    venting are not bureaucratic boxes. Each of them exists because of accidents. An inspector
    looking over the work is the cheapest second opinion you will ever get on a gas appliance in
    your home.
  </p>

  <h2>How the Process Goes on a Normal Job</h2>
  <ol class="steps">
    <li><strong>Permit at the time of work</strong> The contractor applies with the local building department. For a straight swap this is routine administration, not a design review.</li>
    <li><strong>Install to current code</strong> Current requirements apply even if the old unit predated them &mdash; strapping, relief discharge, pan, expansion control, venting and gas connection all come up to standard.</li>
    <li><strong>Inspection</strong> The inspector visits, works the list above, and signs off. Blocked access to the heater is the most common reason for a return visit.</li>
    <li><strong>Final record</strong> The permit is closed out and the work is on record for the property.</li>
  </ol>

